  "id": "DrugBank_DB13231 - MONDO_745",
  "verbalization": "Calcium Lactate is implicated in the pathogenesis of Cardiac Arrest through a chain of indirect interactions involving phosphorus metabolism. The evidence suggests that Calcium Lactate is linked to Phosphorus Metabolism Disease, which in turn is associated with other calcium salts such as Calcium Chloride, Calcium Acetate, and Calcium Glucoheptonate. These calcium compounds are all involved in the regulation of phosphorus levels, which play a critical role in cellular function and energy metabolism. The presence of these calcium salts in the context of phosphorus metabolism disease may contribute to the disruption of cellular processes that are essential for maintaining cardiac function. While the exact mechanism by which these compounds lead to Cardiac Arrest remains unclear, the integrated evidence points to a potential role of calcium-based compounds in the pathophysiology of cardiac arrest, particularly through their effects on phosphorus homeostasis.",
